    ["Mbpp/111", "Write a function to find the common elements in given nested lists.\nYour code should pass this test:\nassert set(common_in_nested_lists([[12, 18, 23, 25, 45], [7, 12, 18, 24, 28], [1, 5, 8, 12, 15, 16, 18]]))==set([18, 12])", ["pp", 3725, 0, "", "", "ets[0]\n    for s in sets[1:]:\n        common &= s\n        if not common:\n            break\n    \n    return common\n\ndef flatten(lst):\n    result = []\n    for element in lst:\n        if isinstance(element, list):\n            result += flatten(element)\n        else:\n            result.append(element)\n    return result\n```"], ["pp", 847, 0, "", "", "ese cases.\n\nTherefore, the function can be written as such, and that's the solution.\n</think>\n\npython\nCODE\n```python\ndef common_in_nested_lists(nested_lists):\n    if not nested_lists:\n        return set()\n    common = set(nested_lists[0])\n    for lst in nested_lists[1:]:\n        common &= set(lst)\n    return common\n```"], ["pp", 3024, 0, "", "", "nput list is empty, we immediately return an empty set. If any list is empty, the intersection result will naturally be an empty set, as there are no elements to be common.\n\nThis approach ensures that we efficiently determine common elements using set operations, making the solution both optimal and easy to understand."]],
